# JavaScript Livelits (advanced) #

# The livelit that ships built in, ^js, evaluates JavaScript. It is #
# here near the end of the deck because it is the least typical one: #
# most livelits are defined in the program, as the earlier slides #
# show, and need no escape hatch. #

# A livelit is a live GUI that fills a typed hole (Sec. 1, Sec. 2). #
# A use has the livelit's declared expansion type (Sec. 3.1) and #
# means what its model EXPANDS to (Sec. 2.2). In the paper that is #
# always a program, given as quoted code (Sec. 3.2.5). Here that kind #
# is a Macro livelit, which cannot return real code yet: there is no #
# quotation, so its Exp can only be a hole or an IntLit (see the #
# Overview, WHAT DOES NOT RUN YET). ^js instead expands to a value, #
# as a Functional livelit does; that arm is this implementation's #
# own, not the paper's. #

# Invocation: #
# To invoke a livelit, type its name (always prefixed with ^), then #
# space. The context inspector shows which livelits are available #
# and the expansion type of each. #

# Each use keeps its MODEL in the program text itself (Sec. 3.2.1): #
# below, it is the two strings inside ^js(...). The widget stands in #
# for that text; to see the text, right-click the widget and choose #
# Remove Livelit. #

# Livelits are DEFINED IN THE PROGRAM: a module bound to a caret name #
# declares types Model, Action and Expansion and provides init, #
# update, view and expand. init is a COMMAND, an UpdateCmd run when #
# a use is created (Sec. 3.2.1); update and view return COMMANDS, an #
# UpdateCmd and a ViewCmd (Sec. 3.2.4, Sec. 3.2.3); a plain answer is #
# written Pure(...). An optional shape -- Inline(w), Block(w, h), or #
# Tab(w, h) -- sets its footprint. The other slides each define one. #

# One livelit ships built in, and it is NOT such a module: ^js is #
# written in the editor's own implementation language, so it is not #
# checked against the Livelit signature, and its starting model, #
# update and view are plain data and functions there, not commands. #
# Its code is a string in the model, not a splice (Sec. 2.4.2), so #
# unlike a splice, which is scoped at the use site (Sec. 2.4.3), it #
# cannot see your variables. #

# ^js #
# Expansion type: String #
# Expansion: the result string stored by the last Compute click. #
# Model: (String, String) -- the code and its result. #
# Compute runs the code in the browser and stores its result in the #
# model. Running the program does not run JavaScript: expand just #
# reads that stored string back out of the model. #
let greeting = ^^livelit(^js("['hi', 'from js'].join(' ')", "hi from js")) in
greeting
